1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,866
Picture the open ocean, roughly ten million years ago.

2
00:00:03,866 --> 00:00:07,733
A baleen whale about the length of a school

3
00:00:07,733 --> 00:00:11,599
bus is cruising near the surface. It has little

4
00:00:11,599 --> 00:00:15,466
reason to fear the sharks around it. But something

5
00:00:15,466 --> 00:00:19,332
is rising from the dark water below. Something with

6
00:00:19,332 --> 00:00:21,480
teeth longer than your forearm.

7
00:00:21,780 --> 00:00:25,646
This is Livyatan, a giant relative of the modern

8
00:00:25,646 --> 00:00:29,512
sperm whale, and one of the most powerful predators

9
00:00:29,512 --> 00:00:33,379
ever to hunt with teeth. In the next few

10
00:00:33,379 --> 00:00:37,245
minutes we will look at how it was found,

11
00:00:37,245 --> 00:00:41,111
how it was built, what it ate, and why

12
00:00:41,111 --> 00:00:41,970
it disappeared.

13
00:00:42,270 --> 00:00:46,135
Our story starts not in the sea, but in

14
00:00:46,135 --> 00:00:50,000
a desert. The Pisco Formation on the southern coast

15
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,866
of Peru is one of the richest marine fossil

16
00:00:53,866 --> 00:00:57,731
sites on Earth. This dry, wind-scoured land was once

17
00:00:57,731 --> 00:01:01,596
seafloor, and today it gives up the bones of

18
00:01:01,596 --> 00:01:05,461
whales, seals, sharks, and even sloths that swam in

19
00:01:05,461 --> 00:01:06,320
the sea.

20
00:01:06,620 --> 00:01:10,488
In 2008, a team working at a site called

21
00:01:10,488 --> 00:01:14,356
Cerro Colorado uncovered a skull. Not a small one.

22
00:01:14,356 --> 00:01:18,223
It measured about three metres (ten feet) from end

23
00:01:18,223 --> 00:01:22,091
to end, and both its upper and lower jaws

24
00:01:22,091 --> 00:01:23,810
still held massive teeth.

25
00:01:24,110 --> 00:01:27,974
The team, led by palaeontologist Olivier Lambert, described the

26
00:01:27,974 --> 00:01:31,838
animal in 2010. They first named it Leviathan, after

27
00:01:31,838 --> 00:01:35,701
the sea monster of legend. But that name had

28
00:01:35,701 --> 00:01:39,565
already been given to another animal, an extinct relative

29
00:01:39,565 --> 00:01:43,429
of elephants, so the genus became Livyatan, the Hebrew

30
00:01:43,429 --> 00:01:47,292
form of the word. The species name, melvillei, honours

31
00:01:47,292 --> 00:01:51,156
Herman Melville, the author of Moby-Dick. A fitting tribute

32
00:01:51,156 --> 00:01:55,020
for a whale that may have hunted other whales.

33
00:01:55,320 --> 00:01:59,187
So how big was it? Only the skull and

34
00:01:59,187 --> 00:02:03,053
teeth are known, so the body has to be

35
00:02:03,053 --> 00:02:06,920
estimated by comparison with living sperm whales. Most estimates

36
00:02:06,920 --> 00:02:10,786
fall between 13.5 and 17.5 metres, or roughly 44

37
00:02:10,786 --> 00:02:14,653
to 57 feet. That is in the same league

38
00:02:14,653 --> 00:02:17,660
as a large male sperm whale today.

39
00:02:17,960 --> 00:02:21,825
The resemblance ends at the mouth. A modern sperm

40
00:02:21,825 --> 00:02:25,690
whale has a long, narrow lower jaw and feeds

41
00:02:25,690 --> 00:02:29,555
mainly by suction, swallowing squid in the deep. Its

42
00:02:29,555 --> 00:02:33,420
upper jaw has few working teeth. Livyatan was different.

43
00:02:33,420 --> 00:02:37,285
It carried big, interlocking teeth in both jaws, set

44
00:02:37,285 --> 00:02:41,151
in a short, wide snout built for gripping and

45
00:02:41,151 --> 00:02:41,580
tearing.

46
00:02:41,880 --> 00:02:45,750
And those teeth were enormous. The largest were more

47
00:02:45,750 --> 00:02:49,620
than 36 centimetres, or about 14 inches, long. Leaving

48
00:02:49,620 --> 00:02:53,490
tusks aside, they are among the largest biting teeth

49
00:02:53,490 --> 00:02:55,640
of any animal ever discovered.

50
00:02:55,940 --> 00:02:59,803
On top of the skull sits a deep hollow

51
00:02:59,803 --> 00:03:03,667
called the supracranial basin. In living sperm whales, a

52
00:03:03,667 --> 00:03:07,530
similar basin holds the spermaceti organ, so Livyatan probably

53
00:03:07,530 --> 00:03:11,394
had one too. What that organ did is still

54
00:03:11,394 --> 00:03:15,257
debated. It may have focused sound for echolocation, helping

55
00:03:15,257 --> 00:03:19,121
the whale find prey in murky water. Some researchers

56
00:03:19,121 --> 00:03:22,984
have even suggested it worked as a cushion for

57
00:03:22,984 --> 00:03:25,560
ramming, although that idea is controversial.

58
00:03:25,860 --> 00:03:29,728
With jaws like these, Livyatan was almost certainly not

59
00:03:29,728 --> 00:03:33,595
slurping up squid. Its most likely prey were baleen

60
00:03:33,595 --> 00:03:37,463
whales, which were common in these seas. Many were

61
00:03:37,463 --> 00:03:41,331
medium-sized, perhaps seven to ten metres, or 23 to

62
00:03:41,331 --> 00:03:42,620
33 feet, long.

63
00:03:42,920 --> 00:03:46,786
The teeth themselves offer clues. Many are heavily worn

64
00:03:46,786 --> 00:03:50,652
where upper and lower teeth ground past each other,

65
00:03:50,652 --> 00:03:54,519
the mark of an animal that bit hard and

66
00:03:54,519 --> 00:03:58,385
often. A large hollow on the side of the

67
00:03:58,385 --> 00:04:02,251
skull anchored the jaw-closing muscles, hinting at a very

68
00:04:02,251 --> 00:04:03,110
powerful bite.

69
00:04:03,410 --> 00:04:07,275
A likely strategy was to approach from below or

70
00:04:07,275 --> 00:04:11,141
behind, seize the prey with a short, crushing bite,

71
00:04:11,141 --> 00:04:15,006
and hold on until it weakened. Nobody has watched

72
00:04:15,006 --> 00:04:18,871
Livyatan hunt, so this is a reconstruction, but it

73
00:04:18,871 --> 00:04:22,736
fits the anatomy. In a way, it played a

74
00:04:22,736 --> 00:04:26,602
role similar to killer whales today, only on a

75
00:04:26,602 --> 00:04:27,890
far larger scale.

76
00:04:28,190 --> 00:04:32,058
Livyatan was not the only giant in those waters.

77
00:04:32,058 --> 00:04:35,926
The same rocks in Peru also contain teeth of

78
00:04:35,926 --> 00:04:39,793
megalodon, the enormous prehistoric shark. The two predators probably

79
00:04:39,793 --> 00:04:43,661
overlapped in time and place, and both seem to

80
00:04:43,661 --> 00:04:45,380
have targeted mid-sized whales.

81
00:04:45,680 --> 00:04:49,546
Did they fight? There is no direct evidence of

82
00:04:49,546 --> 00:04:53,412
that. It is more likely that they competed for

83
00:04:53,412 --> 00:04:57,278
the same food. It is tempting to imagine an

84
00:04:57,278 --> 00:05:01,144
epic battle between them, but the fossils only tell

85
00:05:01,144 --> 00:05:05,010
us that they were neighbours, not who would have

86
00:05:05,010 --> 00:05:05,440
won.

87
00:05:05,740 --> 00:05:09,610
So why did Livyatan vanish? We do not have

88
00:05:09,610 --> 00:05:13,480
a clear answer. Its fossil record is thin, and

89
00:05:13,480 --> 00:05:17,350
the best-dated remains come from a narrow slice of

90
00:05:17,350 --> 00:05:20,790
time around nine to ten million years ago.

91
00:05:21,090 --> 00:05:24,953
One idea is that its menu changed. As the

92
00:05:24,953 --> 00:05:28,815
climate cooled towards the end of the Miocene, ocean

93
00:05:28,815 --> 00:05:32,678
ecosystems were reshaped. Many of the mid-sized baleen whales

94
00:05:32,678 --> 00:05:36,541
it relied on declined, while others grew larger. A

95
00:05:36,541 --> 00:05:40,404
specialist predator can struggle when its prey changes. Isolated

96
00:05:40,404 --> 00:05:44,266
teeth from places such as Chile, South Africa and

97
00:05:44,266 --> 00:05:48,129
Australia suggest giant raptorial sperm whales were once widespread,

98
00:05:48,129 --> 00:05:51,992
but in the end this lineage of whale hunters

99
00:05:51,992 --> 00:05:52,850
faded away.

100
00:05:53,150 --> 00:05:57,018
Today, the sperm whale is the last giant of

101
00:05:57,018 --> 00:06:00,886
its family, a deep-diving squid hunter rather than a

102
00:06:00,886 --> 00:06:04,754
killer of whales. But long ago, its cousin Livyatan

103
00:06:04,754 --> 00:06:08,622
cruised the coast of South America as one of

104
00:06:08,622 --> 00:06:12,490
the most formidable predators the oceans have ever seen.

105
00:06:12,790 --> 00:06:16,663
If you enjoyed this, subscribe for more stories of

106
00:06:16,663 --> 00:06:20,536
giant and strange animals from deep time. And tell

107
00:06:20,536 --> 00:06:24,409
us in the comments: which prehistoric predator should we

108
00:06:24,409 --> 00:06:25,270
explore next?
